{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"MATERIAL FOR THE ADR, discovered by executing J7 [rebar:b4e3-1de0-3188-40a1] and driving the DC\nassembly against a REAL Jira instance for the first time. This is the epic's most transferable lesson\nand the ADR should record it, because it is a property of the SEAM, not of the DC adapter.\n\nTHE FINDING: the Backend port does not state what the core actually requires.\n\n  * 'TicketTransport' declares SIX members: create_issue, get_issue, update_issue,\n    transition_issue_by_name, add_label, search_issues.\n  * The core reconciler calls FIFTEEN distinct methods on a transport.\n  * TEN of them are declared on NO Protocol anywhere in _backend.py: delete_issue, get_comments,\n    get_issue_by_rest, get_issue_links, get_parent_map, remove_label, set_entity_property,\n    set_issue_property, set_reporter, validate_assignee_exists.\n  * All ten are implemented for Cloud (across acli_rest.py, acli.py, acli_graph.py); none on DC.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, measured not theorised: a DC writing reconcile pass crashes with\n'JiraDataCenterTransport object has no attribute set_entity_property' (CI run 30604756524) — while\nisinstance(backend, Backend) passes, the backend contract suite passes, and 1636 reconciler unit tests\npass. Filed as [rebar:a357-b747-ece9-4cf5], fixed by J9 [rebar:bda8-370d-23ad-4e36].\n\nWHY IT BELONGS IN AN ADR RATHER THAN A BUG REPORT: ADR 0035's 'proof-of-seam' argument is that the\nbackend-agnostic contract suite is what certifies a backend. This epic shows that argument holds only\nas far as the port is COMPLETE. A contract suite can only test what the port declares, so an\nunder-declared port yields a certificate that means less than it reads as — and it fails in the most\nexpensive direction, silently, at runtime, on the second backend rather than the first. The GitHub\nadapter (epic be74) will hit exactly this unless the ADR names it.\n\nSUGGESTED ADR CONTENT: state that a port's declared surface must be derived from the core's actual\ncall sites and kept honest by a STRUCTURAL TEST (J9 adds one), not by authorial discipline; and that\n'the contract suite certifies a backend' is conditional on that. Also worth recording that the defect\nwas invisible to every static and unit-level signal and was caught only by a live pass — which is the\nevidence for keeping a live tier in the seam's verification story at all.\n\nRELATED, also for the ADR's provenance section: the shared-jira decision this epic makes was found to\nbe only PARTLY implemented. RemoteRef.instance — the field the decision leans on to distinguish\ndeployments — is NEVER CONSTRUCTED in production code (src/ has only the dataclass definition and\ndocstrings; the sole constructions are two test helpers). Filed as [rebar:6a91-7429-e521-4a2e]. The\nADR should not assert deployments ARE distinguished by RemoteRef.instance as though it were built;\nstate it as the intended design with the gap named, or the next reader will rely on a field nothing\npopulates."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785475727714930001,"uuid":"b18f0595-3a93-4253-b0eb-7468011b933b"}